window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2026-01-20"]={"AD-001-26":"Resolution No. AD-001-26\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nAUTHORIZATION FOR REAL PROPERTY TAX\nREFUNDS/CORRECTIONS - 2026\nWHEREAS, New York State Real Property Tax Law, Sections 554 and 556, provides for an administrative correction of error process that assists property owners in obtaining refunds and/or corrections for real property tax payments or billings, and\nWHEREAS, certain clerical errors, unlawful entries and errors in essential fact, as defined in the New York State Real Property Tax Law, are permitted to be corrected by a taxing jurisdiction based upon the recommendation of the Director of Real Property Tax Services, and\nWHEREAS, the taxing jurisdiction can delegate, by resolution, on an annual basis, to an official who is empowered to authorize payment of bills without prior audit, the authority to perform the duties of the tax levying body in regard to Real Property Tax Law, Sections 554 and 556, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Treasurer is hereby delegated the authority to perform the duties of the tax levying body during 2026 when the Real Property Tax Director's recommended refund and/or correction is twenty five hundred dollars or less, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that on or before the fifteenth day of each month, the Niagara County Treasurer shall submit a report to the Niagara County Legislature of the refunds and/or corrections processed during the preceding month, containing the name of each recipient, the location of the property, and the amount of the refund.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-002-26":"Resolution No. AD-002-26\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nABOLISH/CREATE POSITIONS RISK AND INSURANCE SERVICES DEPARTMENT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature desires to restructure the composition of the leadership of the Risk and Insurance Services Department, and\nWHEREAS, there is a vacant Principal Insurance Program Assistant and vacant Senior Insurance Program Assistant position in the Department, and\nWHEREAS, it has been determined that the duties to be performed are more appropriately classified as Insurance Program Assistant II and the Principal Insurance Program Assistant position is not needed at this time, and\nWHEREAS, it has been determined that a Risk Manager position is necessary to provide oversight of the daily operations of the Department, and\nWHEREAS, it has been determined that an Insurance Plans Administrator position is necessary to provide expert technical guidance to Niagara County leadership, and\nWHEREAS, the restructuring of the Department pursuant to this resolution would result in a projected cost savings this year of approximately $56,570, and\nWHEREAS, the Legislature desires to enter into an employment agreement with Jennifer Pitarresi who possesses the technical expertise needed to perform the duties of the newly created Insurance Plans Administrator, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Principal Insurance Program Assistant position #13915 and Senior Insurance Program Assistant position #13278 will be abolished effective January 26, 2026, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a department head Risk Manager position at Flat salary grade 13 $83,605 - $105,932 and an Insurance Program Assistant II position at CSEA grade 8 will be created effective January 26, 2026, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that a flat salary FLSA exempt Insurance Plans Administrator at $85,000 will be created effective January 26, 2026, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the County Attorney finalize an employment agreement with Jennifer Pitarresi to perform the duties of the newly created Insurance Plans Administrator position.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-001-26":"Resolution No. CS-001-26\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nMH - REQUEST FOR WAIVER FROM THE NIAGARA COUNTY\nPURCHASING PROCEDURES FOR PROFESSIONAL SERVICES\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health enters into contracts with not-for-profit agencies for the provision of mental health, alcohol and substance abuse, and developmental disabilities services, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Office of Alcoholism and Substance Abuse Services (OASAS) provides funding for the jail-based substance use disorder treatment, including medication assisted treatment, and transition services in Niagara County Jail, and\nWHEREAS, Peaceprints of WNY currently provides pre-release and post release services for inmates in the Niagara County Jail through their Project Blue program, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office desires to have streamlined services, communication, and coordination of pre-release and post-release services, including for individuals with substance use disorder, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Guidelines allow for waiver of Requests for Proposal for professional services contracts where justification exists, and\nWHEREAS, waiver of Requests for Proposal will serve the interests of Niagara County and its taxpayers by minimizing the interruption of services between previous provider and a new provider and streamlining operations within the jail setting with a provider who has a strong track record of providing quality services, maintaining communication, effective coordination and fiscal accountability, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature has already approved the budget of $266,555 for Jail-Based Services (screening, assessment, counseling, discharge planning, medication assisted treatment, and follow-up) in the adoption of the 2026 County Budget so no budget modification will be necessary, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of Community Services is hereby authorized to engage in the process to carry out the necessmy agreements between the County and Peaceprints of WNY as aforesaid, subject to the approval of the County Attorney's Office.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-001-26":"Resolution No. CSS-001-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nAW ARD OF 911 SYSTEM HARDWARE REFRESH WITH SUPPORT FOR\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE SEALED BID\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Department issued Sealed Bid #2025-53 - 911 System Hardware Refresh with Support for Niagara County Sheriffs Office on December 4, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, only one response was received for the bid opening on December 23, 2025, which was from Kraus Associates, Inc. d/b/a AK Associates, PO Box 2880, Concord, OH 03302, in the amount of $430,506.93, and\nWHEREAS, the proposal from Kraus Associates, Inc. d/b/a AK Associates has been evaluated for compliance with the Sealed Bid parameters and appropriateness of cost, and\nWHEREAS, the 911 System Hardware Refresh project is being funded through a New York State Department of Homeland Security SFY2025 Next Generation 911 (NG911) Grant, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Sealed Bid #2025-53 - 911 System Hardware Refresh with Support for Niagara County Sheriffs Office be awarded to Kraus Associates, Inc. d/b/a AK Associates, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Sealed Bid #2025-53 - 911 System Hardware Refresh with Support for Niagara County Sheriffs Office contract with Kraus Associates, Inc. d/b/a AK Associates, may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-002-26":"Resolution No. CSS-002-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nA WARD OF SEALED BID REMEDIATION OF DUCT WORK\nAND CEILING FOR NIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Department issued Sealed Bid #2025-52 Remediation of Duct Work and Ceiling for Niagara County Sheriffs Office on October 30, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, only one response was received for the bid opening on November 25, 2025, which was from D.V. Brown & Associates, Inc., 567 Vickers Street, Tonawanda, NY 14150 in the amount of$135,000.00, and\nWHEREAS, the proposal from D.V. Brown & Associates, Inc. has been evaluated for compliance with the Sealed Bid parameters and appropriateness of cost and is being funded through Capital Project H813 Jail Building Improvements, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Sealed Bid #2025-52 Remediation of Duct Work and Ceiling for Niagara County Sheriffs Office be awarded to D.V. Brown & Associates, Inc., and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Sealed Bid #2025-52 Remediation of Duct Work and Ceiling for Niagara County Sheriffs Office contract with D.V. Brown & Associates, Inc. may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-003-26":"Resolution No. CSS-003-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nABOLISH COMPUTER PROGRAMMER POSITION AND CREATE AND FILL\nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY TECHNICIAN POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified of the upcoming retirement of their Computer Programmer as of February 13, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office has analyzed the current Information Technology needs of the Office and has determined that the Information Technology Technician position is more appropriate for the current demands of the department and agencies that the Sheriffs Office supports, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution No. AD-014-23 on May 16, 2023, to grant residency waivers when there is a demonstrated recruitment difficulty for all Information Technology technical job classifications while continuing to give Niagara County residents preference in hiring decisions, and\nWHEREAS, there will be position savings in 2026 by abolishing a Computer Programmer CSEA Group 11 Step 8 position to create an Information Technology Technician CSEA Group 12 Step I position, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Computer Programmer Position #4071 CSEA Group 11 Step 8 at $39.54 per hour be abolished as of March 14, 2026, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that one Information Technology Technician position be created and filled in Job Group 12 Step 1 at a rate of $36.64 per hour effective March 15, 2026, with a residency waiver, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00  Position expense  $60,830\nComputer Programmer (#4071)\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00  Position Expense  $60,830\nInformation Technology Technician\n(position# new)\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-004-26":"Resolution No. CSS-004-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - POLICE TRAFFIC SERVICES GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution No. CSS-128-25 on December 2, 2025, to accept the Police Traffic Services Grant from the Governor's Traffic Safety Committee for the period October 1, 2025 through September 30, 2026, in the amount of$25,810, and\nWHEREAS, this grant is a continuation of an existing program and is fully funded by the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are used for traffic enforcement overtime with the goal of increasing seat belt usage and reducing dangerous driving behaviors in an effort to reduce serious injuries and deaths from traffic crashes in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the 2026 Budget needs to be modified in order to utilize the balance of the grant funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3110.000 44389.09  Other Public Safety Traffic  $23,698\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71050.00  Overtime Expense  $23,698\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-005-26":"Resolution No. CSS-005-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - SFY2025 NEXT GENERATION 911 GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution No. CSS-113-25 on October 21, 2025, to accept the SFY2025 Next Generation 911 (NG91 l) Grant from the New York State Department of Homeland Security and Emergency Services for the period September 1, 2025 through August 31, 2030, in the amount of $1,639,396, and\nWHEREAS, this program funding is offered on a one-time basis to allow counties to enhance their public safety operations, including strengthening 911 communication infrastructure focusing on the overarching goal of increasing capabilities to receive and relay 911 calls in accordance with the current National Emergency Number Association (NENA) i3 standard, and\nWHEREAS, the 2026 Budget needs to be modified in order to utilize the balance of the grant funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 43305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $1,239,396\nProject 17GRTNG911-Rl\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 72100.15  Communications Equipment  $1,239,936\nProject l 7GRTNG911-El\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-006-26":"Resolution No. CSS-006-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nSOLE SOURCE PURCHASE OF COMMERCIAL LOAD WEIGHING DEVICES\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has identified an issue with excessive weight trucks on County roadways and would like the Niagara County Sheriffs Office to be involved in weighing vehicles as part of our Commercial Vehicle Law Enforcement, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office needs to purchase four portable hydraulic/analog low profile wheel loader weighers to aid in the weight enforcement of Commercial Vehicles, and\nWHEREAS, the Haenni WL101 wheel load weigher is the only hydraulic/analog low profile wheel load weigher in production in the world, protected under United States Patent #4,673,049, and\nWHEREAS, Loadometer Corporation is the Haenni & Cie.AG's sole source in the United States since 1979 for the sale, parts and service of their Wheel Load Weighing devices, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has identified available Asset Forfeiture funds available to purchase four ( 4) Wheel Load Weighing devices, and\nWHEREAS, the 2026 Budget needs to be modified in order to utilize the Asset Forfeiture funds for this purchase, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that under New York State Common Law there is a need for sole source purchase for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the purchase of four (4) Haenni WL101 hydraulic/analog low profile wheel loader weighers as the good/service can only be obtained through a single source with no possibility of competition, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that Loadometer Corporation is the sole source of the Haenni WL!0l wheel load weighers and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase Haenni WL101 wheel load weighers from Loadometer Corporation without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3110.000 44389.12  Other Public Safety Asset Forfeiture\nDept. Treasury  $23,980\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 72100.32  Machinery and Equipment Asset Forfeiture\nDept. Treasury  $23,980\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-007-26":"Resolution No. CSS-007-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nCREATE AND FILL TWO TEMPORARY DEPUTY SHERIFF POSITIONS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified of the retirement of a Deputy Sheriff Captain and a Deputy Sheriff in March of 2026, and\nWHEREAS, the next Niagara County Law Enforcement Academy will start in February, 2026, and the following Academy will not start until August, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, new Deputy Sheriffs go through a six-month Law Enforcement Academy followed by two months of Field Training before they are able to operate on their own, and\nWHEREAS, due to the lengthy training needed for a new Deputy Sheriff, it is important to start new Deputies in the February Academy in anticipation of these retirements, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that two temporary Deputy Sheriff positions be created and filled effective 2/1/2026 at PBA Deputy Sheriff Step 2 at an hourly rate of $32.04/hour, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the temporary positions will be abolished upon the Deputy Sheriff vacancies from the retirements, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00 Posn # 380  Position Expense  $11,000\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00 Posn # 6236  Position Expense  $ 6,950\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00 Posn # NEW  Position Expense  $ 8,975\nA.17.3110.000 71010.00 Posn # NEW  Position Expense  $ 8,975\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-008-26":"Resolution No. CSS-008-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nFEDERAL EQUITABLE SHARING ANNUAL RECERTIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the United States Department of Justice requires that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office file an annual Federal Equitable Sharing Program Agreement and Certification Report in order to remain eligible to submit requests for federal forfeited property or proceeds, and\nWHEREAS, the Agreement is entered into by the United States Department of Justice, the County of Niagara and the Niagara County Sheriffs Office, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be authorized to continue to participate in the Federal Equitable Sharing Program, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the United States Department of Justice Federal Equitable Sharing Agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-009-26":"Resolution No. CSS-009-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nACCEPT IGNITION INTERLOCK DEVICE MONITORING\nPROGRAM AW ARD FOR 2025-2026 - PROBATION DEPARTMENT\nWHEREAS, the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services has made an award to Niagara County for the Ignition Interlock Device Monitoring Program for term dates October I, 2025 through September 30, 2026,and\nWHEREAS, the Grant is being renewed in the amount of$33,014.14, and said funds are used to offset expenses for monitoring Ignition Interlock Device installation by offenders, and the revenue has been budgeted for in the 2025 and 2026 budgets, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the grant agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the grant agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-010-26":"Resolution No. CSS-010-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPTANCE OF NIAGARA COUNTY\nPRETRIAL SERVICES AWARD GRANT-PROBATION DEPARTMENT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Probation Department has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services of an award of funding to offset the costs associated with the provision of certified pretrial services in the amount of $772,096, and\nWHEREAS, funding will be utilized by the Probation Department for pretrial services including but not limited to, screening, assessment, supervision, and reporting as provided in the enacted NYS budget, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the grant agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Probation Department is given authorization to accept the grant, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following 2026 budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.18.3140.000 43310.02  Probation Services Alternatives to  $104,000\nIncarceration\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.18.3140.000 72100.05  Machinery and Equipment, Computer  $15,000\nEquipment\nA.18.3140.000 72100.21  Machinery and Equipment, LE Equipment  $45,000\nA.18.3140.000 74250.01  Office Expenses/Office Supplies  $3,000\nA.18.3140.000 74300.01  Reimbursements Travel Conference  $2,000\nA.18.3140.000 74450.02  Special Activities Safety/Wellness  $21,000\nA.18/3140.000 74500.02  Contractual Expenses, Maintenance  $10,000\nService Contracts\nA.18.3140.000 74750.05  Supplies, General LE Supplies  $6,000\nA.18.3140.000 74800.11  Supplies/Services, Vehicle Maintenance  $2,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-011-26":"Resolution No. CSS-011-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY OFFICE OF EMERGENCY SERVICES\n2026 BUDGET MODIFICATION ROLLOVER 2025 HOMELAND SECURITY UNSPENT FUNDS\nWHEREAS, Homeland Security funds were not fully expended in 2025, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the remaining funds for 2023 State Homeland Security Program (approved #CSS-114-23) and 2024 State Homeland Security Program (approved Resolution No. CSS-112-24) be re-appropriated into the 2026 budget, and be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the remaining funds for 2024 Emergency Management Preparedness Grant (approved\nResolution No. CSS-113-24), be re-appropriated into the 2026 budget and county contributions be re-appropriated from the Fund Balance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following 2026 budget modification be effectuated effective immediately: INCREASE REVENUE: ** SHSP23**\nA.19.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $192,650.63\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:  **SHSP23**\nA.19.3645.000 72100.14  M&E Misc. Equipment  $ 30,170.86\nA.19.3645.000 74300.01  Reimbursements Travel, Conference  $ 3,647.28\nA.19.3645.000 74500.01  Contractual Expense  $ 34,041.90\nA.19.3645.000 74500.02  Contractual Maint. Expense  $ 75,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74750.02  General Supplies  $ 3,347.74\nA.19.3645.000 74800.01  Communication Services  $ 15,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74800.10  Misc. Equipment <$500  $ 31,442.85\nINCREASE REVENUE: ** SHSP24**\nA.19.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $283,870.29\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION: **SHSP24**\nA.19.3645.000 72100.14  M&E Misc. Equipment  $ 55,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74300.01  Reimbursements Travel, Conference  $ 20,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74500.01  Contractual Expense  $ 25,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74500.02  Contractual Maint. Expense  $135,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74750.02  General Supplies  $ 2,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74800.01  Communication Services  $ 5,000.00\nA.19.3645.000 74800.10  Misc. Equipment <$500  $ 41,870.29\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION FUND BALANCE - ASSIGNED: **EMPG24**\nA.40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $  314.04\nINCREASE REVENUE: ** EMPG24**\nA.19.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $  314.04\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION: ** EMPG24**\nA.19.3645.000 72100.11  M&E Other Vehicles  $  628.08\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-012-26":"Resolution No. CSS-012-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nREGIONAL AGREEMENT BETWEEN NIAGARA AND ERIE COUNTY\nTO PERFORM AUTOPSY SERVICES\nWHEREAS, Niagara County and Erie County entered into an agreement in 2005 that allows the Erie County Medical Examiner's Office to perform all of Niagara County's autopsies, which agreement has subsequently been renewed, and\nWHEREAS, the cun-ent agreement expired on December 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, Erie County has offered us a one-year agreement at $2,616/full autopsy, $2,049/partial autopsy exam and $1,512/external exam, along with other associated fees, and\nWHEREAS, it is beneficial for Niagara County to continue this practice and promote regionalism and shared services between the counties of Niagara and Erie, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the County of Niagara does hereby agree to the terms and conditions in said agreement and authorizes the Chaitman of the Legislature to execute the contract with Erie County and the Erie County Medical Examiner's Office.\nMoved by McKimmie, seconded by Casale, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to refen-ing it to committee. Can-ied.\nMoved by McKimmie, seconded by Casale.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-013-26":"Resolution No. CSS-013-26\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nWAIVER OF RESIDENCY REQUEST\nASSISTANT PUBLIC DEFENDER POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Defender Office has permission to fill a vacant Assistant Public Defender position, this is a critical and important position that is required to satisfy operational requirements of the department and to provide effective legal representation to indigent defendants charged of crimes, and\nWHEREAS, due to challenges with the recruitment for qualified candidates despite posting efforts and activities to source candidates for this important position has not resulted in a sufficient applicant pool of Niagara County resident candidates applying for this vacant position, and\nWHEREAS, Public Defender Office has made diligent and sincere efforts to recruit highly qualified candidates for the position from within Niagara County, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a one-time Waiver of Residency from the policy to hire only Niagara County residents be\napproved to fill the currently vacant Assistant Public Defender position #13820 as there are no remaining Niagara County residents in the applicant pool.\nMoved by McKimmie, seconded by Robins, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to refen-ing it to committee. Can-ied.\nMoved by McKimmie, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-001-26":"Resolution No. ED-001-26\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nAPPROVING THE INCLUSION OF VIABLE AGRICULTURAL LAND WITHIN\nCERTIFIED AGRICULTURAL DISTRICTS PURSUANT TO SECTION 303-B\nOF THE NEW YORK STATE AGRICULTURE AND MARKETS LAW\nWHEREAS, Section 303-b of Agriculture and Markets Law provides land owners with an annual thirty-day period to submit requests for the inclusion of predominantly viable agricultural lands within certified agricultural districts, and\nWHEREAS, that thirty-day period began June 1, 2025, and ended June 30, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, land owners have filed a request for inclusion of predominantly viable agricultural land within the certified agricultural district, and\nWHEREAS, the County Legislature referred the requests to the Niagara Connty Agricultural and Farmland Protection Board, and that Board has reviewed the requests and provided its recommendations that such property consists predominantly of viable agricultural land and that the inclusion of such land would serve the public interest by assisting in maintaining a viable agricultural industry within the district, and\nWHEREAS, a public hearing was held on Tuesday, October 21, 2025 at 5:45p.m. relating to such requested inclusions, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that based on the recommendations of the Agricultural and Farmland Protection Board, this Niagara County Legislature hereby adopts and approves the inclusion of the requested viable agricultural land within cettified Agricultural District #7, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that in accordance with Section 303-b of Agriculture and Markets Law the modifications to Agricultural District #7, are hereby described as follows, and include the following parcels listed by agricultural district, town, owner, tax map number, and acreage: 2025 Addition Recommendation:\nAgricultural District Town  Owner  Tax Map Number  Acreage\n7  Lockport  Rosann Berry  136.00-1-49.11  24.13\n7  Lockport  Rosann Berry  136.00-1-35  28.06\n7  Lockpott  William Goodine  108.00-1-4.1  23.29\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-002-26":"Resolution No. ED-002-26\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nFIXING DATE AND NOTICE FOR THE PUBLIC HEARING\nFOR MICRO-ENTERPRISE GRANT FUNDS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature wishes to provide information about the progress of the ongoing Community Development Block Grant (CDBG) microenterprise program, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is required to hold a public hearing to provide information to the public and to consider citizens' views on proposed activities and regarding community needs, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature shall conduct a public hearing whereat all interested parties shall be heard regarding the CDBG microenterprise program, and the County's community development and economic development needs at the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, 175 Hawley Street, Lockport, New York on the 17th day of February, 2026 at 5:50 p.m., and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature, at least eight (8) days in advance of such hearing, shall post a notice on the Niagara County Legislature website, and shall publish such notices once in the Lockport Union Sun & Journal and the Niagara Gazette.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-001-26":"Resolution No. IF-001-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nCAPITAL PROJECT CREATION\nWATER TREATMENT PLANT COAGULATION BASIN RESTORATION\nWATER DISTRICT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Water District (\"NCWD\") recognizes the need for a new project for coagulation basin restoration at the water treatment plant with an estimated total project cost of $1,500,000, and\nWHEREAS, there is sufficient funds in the Water District's Fund Balance to cover the cost of this project, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the NCWD hereby establishes an appropriation of $1,500,000 from its Fund Balance for the coagulation basin restoration project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the NCWD hereby requests appropriate action from the Niagara County Legislature, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following 2026 budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nFX.40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $1,500,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nFX.31.9950.000 79010.00  Transfer to Capital Construction  $1,500,000\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH816.31.8397.000 45031.00  Interfund Transfer, From Operating  $1,500,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH816.3 l.8397.000 72200.0 I  WTP Coagulation Basin Restoration  $1,500,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-002-26":"Resolution No. IF-002-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nCAPITAL PROJECT CREATION\nTRANSMISSION MAIN INTERCONNECTIONS - WATER DISTRICT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Water District (\"NCWD\") recognizes the need for a new supply\ninterconnection project with an estimated total project cost of $4,000,000, and\nWHEREAS, there are sufficient funds in the Water District's Capital Reserves to cover the cost of this project,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the NCWD hereby establishes an appropriation of $4,000,000 from its Capital Reserve Fund for the new supply interconnection project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the NCWD hereby requests appropriate action by the Niagara County Legislature, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following 2026 budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH817.31.8397.000 45031.10  Interfund Transfers from Capital Reserves  $4,000,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED RESERVE:\nFXH 40511.00  Appropriated Capital Reserve  $4,000,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nFXH.31.9950.000 79010.00  Transfer to Capital Construction  $4,000,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH817.3 l .8397.000 72600.03  Transmission Interconnections  $4,000,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-003-26":"Resolution No. IF-003-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nAPPROVAL TO ADOPT, PUBLISH & ENFORCE\nNIAGARA COUNTY ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE POLICY\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Information Technology (IT) drafted a policy for governing the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) technology by county departments and employees, and\nWHEREAS, the County departments and employees desiring to use Generative AI and AI enabled technology to enhance their work product and services to county residents will be granted access to the application or service in accordance with the AI Policy, and\nWHEREAS, after this date, the AI Policy shall be in effect thereby making it required that any department and or employee requesting access shall do so in writing tln\u00b7ough their Department Head; the request shall flow through Niagara County IT and the AI Review Board made up of Risk Management, Human Resources and the County Attorney, and\nWHEREAS, prior to granting access to the approved Generative AI technology and or AI enabled technology, the requestor shall complete required training issued by Niagara County IT, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Artificial Intelligence Policy be published and made available for review through the Niagara County Intranet, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this policy shall be reviewed and updated annually or when deemed necessary by the AI Review Board.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-004-26":"Resolution No. IF-004-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nAW ARD CONSUL TANT CONTRACT - NATURAL PLAYGROUND PHASE II PROJECT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated the qualifications of Fisher Associates to design the Phase 2 of the Natural Playground, and\nWHEREAS, Fisher Associates previously designed Phase 1 of the Natural Playground, and\nWHEREAS, Fisher Associates was previously approved as qualified in a prior term agreement evaluation review, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H814.15.7110.000 72400.00, Land Improvements, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Natural Playground Phase II Project be awarded to Fisher Associates, 180 Charlotte Street, Rochester, NY 14067 for a contract amount not to exceed $62,000, and\nbe it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-005-26":"Resolution No. IF-005-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT - EWINGS ROAD/18 MILE CREEK AND\nQUAKER ROAD OVER GOLDEN HILL CREEK\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Ewings Road/18 Mile Creek and Quaker Road over Golden Hill Creek Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account number H765. l 5.5 l 97.000 72600.02, Infrastructure Bridges, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on December 12, 2025 as tabulated below:\nI.  McLaughlin Construction Corp.  $ 1,490,758.84\nDBAEdbauer\n2790 Clinton Street\nWest Seneca, NY 14224\n2.  Hunting Valley Construction  $ 1,971,012.14\n825 Rein Road\nCheektowaga, NY 14225\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Ewings Road/18 Mile Creek and Quaker Road over Golden Hill Creek Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, McLaughlin Construction Corp., DBA Edbauer, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, in the amount of$1,490,758.84, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the agreement for the Ewings Road/18 Mile Creek and Quaker Road over Golden Hill Creek Project may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-006-26":"Resolution No. IF-006-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nGOLF COURSE LOCKER AND RESTROOM ALTERA TIO NS\nCONSULT ANT AMENDMENT NO. 3 FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-150-23, dated October 17, 2023, authorized the contract for consultant services for the Golf Course Locker and Restroom Alterations Project to Nussbaumer & Clarke, 80 Main Street, Unit A, Lockport, NY 14094, for a contract amount not to exceed $55,400, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-170-24, dated October 15, 2024, increased the contract in the amount of$7,050 for additional design services to bid the foundation and slab of the Link Structure separate from the Locker Room Design, for a revised contract amount of $62,450, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-211-24 dated December 3, 2025, increased the contract in the amount of $21,200 for additional engineering services, for a revised contract amount of $83,650, and also extended the contract date to December 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of $18.00, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of $83,632, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Amendment No. 3-Final for Nussbaumer & Clarke, 80 Main Street, Unit A, Lockport, NY 14094, for the Golf Course Locker and Restroom Alterations Project, to reduce the contract in the amount of $18.00, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of$83,632, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-007-26":"Resolution No. IF-007-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nWILSON-BURT ROAD BRIDGE OVER EIGHTEEN MILE CREEK\nCONSUL TANT AMENDMENT NO. 4-FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-114-21, dated November 16, 2021, authorized the contract for consultant services for the Wilson-Burt Road Bridge over Eighteen Mile Creek Project to C & S Engineers, Inc., 499 Col Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, for a contract amount not to exceed $143,000, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-003-23, dated January 17, 2023, authorized Amendment No. 1 to the contract for $95,000 for construction inspection and construction administration, for a revised contract amount of $238,000, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-172-23, dated November 14, 2023, awarded the Wilson-Buti Road Bridge over Eighteen Mile Creek Project to Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, at a reduced base scope of work because the project budget would not support awarding the full base bid amount of the lowest responsible bidder, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-179-23, dated December 5, 2023, authorized Amendment No. 2 to reduce the contract by $41,000, due to the reduced base scope of work, for a revised contract amount of $197,000, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-096-24, dated May 14, 2024, extended the contract completion date to December 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the Contract has been completed to the satisfaction of the Niagara County Department of Public Works, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the agreement with C&S Engineers be closed out.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-008-26":"Resolution No. IF-008-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nPUBLIC SAFETY TRAINING FACILITY\nROOF AND ROOF TOP UNITS REPLACEMENT\nCONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. 2-FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-148-23, dated October 17, 2023, authorized the contract for consultant services for the Public Safety Training Facility Roof and Roof Top Units Replacement Project to DiDonato Associates, 689 Main Street, Buffalo, NY 14203, for a contract amount not to exceed $95,500, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-212-24 extended the contract date to December 31, 2025, at no additional cost to the County, and WHERAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of $40.65 as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of$95,459.35, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Amendment No. 2- Final to DiDonato Associates, 689 Main Street, Buffalo, NY 14203, for the Public Safety Training Facility Roof and Roof Top Units Replacement Project, to reduce the contract amount of$40.65, for a revised contract amount of$95,459.35 be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-009-26":"Resolution No. IF-009-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nBRALEY ROAD OVER THE EAST BRANCH OF TWELVE MILE CREEK BRIDGE\nRAIL DESIGN CONSULTANT AMENDMENT NO. I-FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-073-23 dated April 25, 2023, authorized the consultant services contract for the emergency repairs to the bridge and approach railings of Braley Road over the East Branch of Twelve Mile Creek Project to CHA Consulting Inc., 2200 Main Place Tower, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a contract amount not to exceed $38,500.00, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of$14,665.00, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of$23,835.00, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Amendment No. 1-Final to CHA Consulting Inc., 2200 Main Place Tower, Buffalo, NY 14202, to decrease the contract in the amount of $14,665.00, for the bridge and approach railings of Braley Road over the East Branch of Twelve Mile Creek Project, for the revised contract amount of $23,835.00, be\napproved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-010-26":"Resolution No. IF-010-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nHARTLAND ROAD BRIDGE OVER GOLDEN HILL CREEK\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1 FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-193-24, dated November 12, 2024, authorized the contract for contractor services for the Hattland Road bridge over Golden Hill Creek to Hunting Valley Construction, 825 Rein Road Cheektowaga, NY 14225, in the amount of$827,607.50, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of$52,410.54, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of $775,196.96, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1-Final, to reduce the Hartland Road bridge over Golden Hill Creek contract in the amount of $52,410.54, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of $775,196.96, to Hunting Valley Construction, 825 Rein Road Cheektowaga, NY 14225, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-011-26":"Resolution No. IF-011-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nREHABILITATION OF UPPER MOUNTAIN ROAD FROM ROUTE 31 TO\nLOCKPORT JUNCTION ROAD CHANGE ORDER NO. 1 FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-108-25, dated June 17, 2025, authorized the contract for contractor services for the rehabilitation of Upper Mountain Road from Route 31 to Lockport-Junction Road to Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, in the amount of$2,113,310.83, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of$124,474.30, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of$ I ,988,836.53, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. I-Final to Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, for the rehabilitation of Upper Mountain Road from Route 31 to Lockport-Junction, to reduce the contract in the amount of $124,474.30, for a revised contract amount of $1,988,836.53, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-012-26":"Resolution No. IF-012-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nREPLACEMENT OF HESS ROAD CULVERT NO. 24-24 PROJECT\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 1 FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-187-24, dated November 12, 2024, authorized the contract for contractor services for the replacement of Hess Road Culvert 24-24 to Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY I 4224, in the amount of $1,292,434.25, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to reduce the contract in the amount of $203,892.40, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of$1,088,541.85, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. I-Final to Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, for the replacement of Hess Road Culvert 24-24, to reduce the contract in the amount of $203,892.40, for a revised contract amount of $1,088,541.85, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-013-26":"Resolution No. IF-013-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nSHARED SERVICES AGREEMENT FOR\nSTORMWATER MANAGEMENT COORDINATOR\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works desires to continue the shared services agreement with the City of Tonawanda to assist the County with the compliance of Phase II Stormwater Regulations, and\nWHEREAS, the continuation of the shared services agreement with the City of Tonawanda would be beneficial to both municipalities, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account D.15.5140.000 74650.08, Consultant Services for 2026, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the shared services agreement for the implementation of the Phase II Stormwater Regulations be executed with the City of Tonawanda, 200 Niagara Street, Tonawanda, NY 14150, for a contract amount not to exceed $53,000 for the last renewal period of March 1, 2026, through February 28, 2027, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-014-26":"Resolution No. IF-014-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nDPW-HIGHWAY\nEXPENDITURE OF COUNTY ROAD FUND MONIES\nWHEREAS, the Commissioner of Public Works recommends the expenditure of County Road Fund monies for maintenance of roads under Section 129 of the Highway Law, in the sum of$ 10,754,052, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the necessary documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Recommendation of the Commissioner of Public Works be approved and the sum of $10,754,052 be appropriated from the County Road Fund for maintenance during the year 2026, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the required documents can be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-015-26":"Resolution No. IF-015-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY SECTION 5311\nCONSOLIDATED GRANT ACCEPTANCE OF FUNDS\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara is accepting 5311 consolidated grant of funds from the New York State Department of Transportation, pursuant to Section 5311, Title 49 US Code, for federal financial operating assistance in the amount of $117,300, for 2024, and $117,300, for 2025, for a project to provide a route deviation public transportation service for the non-urbanized areas of Niagara County, including the towns, Cambria, Hartland, Lewiston, Lockport, Newfane, Niagara, Pendleton, Royalton, Somerset, Wilson and the cities of Lockpo1t and Niagara Falls, to be serviced by Niagara Falls Coach Lines, Inc., during the period January 1, 2026, through December 31, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara and the State of New York have entered into a continuing agreement which authorizes the undertaking of the Project and reimbursement of the federal and applicable state share, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara is contracting with a third-party subcontractor, Niagara Falls Coach Lines, Inc., for the project described above, and\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara accept the 5311 consolidated grant funds of $117,300, for the year of 2024, and $117,300, for the year of 2025, Comptroller's Contract No. C005985, for the operation of the County of Niagara's Rural Transportation Service Connect Niagara, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature is authorized to act on behalf of the County of Niagara to sign the application and progress and completed the above-mentioned project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the documentation may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-016-26":"Resolution No. IF-016-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nDEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC WORKS CONSUL TANT CONTRACT EXTENSION\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-113-25 dated June 17, 2025, increased estimated appropriation for Consultant/Expert Service for the prior public works fiscal administrator to $19,999.00, and\nWHEREAS, the Consultant/Expert Service employee had agreed to return as a consultant, for a period of July 1, 2025 through December 31, 2025, for a fee not to exceed $19,999, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Guidelines allow for waiver of Requests for Proposals for professional services contracts where justification exists, and\nWHEREAS, it has been determined that the Niagara County Department of Public Works would like to continue the Consultant/Expert Service agreement for the amount of $19,999.00 and a term of twelve (12) months from January 1, 2026 to December 31, 2026, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature waive the purchasing policy for this professional services contract and allow said Consultant/Expert Service contractor to assist as needed for a time period of twelve (12) months from January I, 2026 through December 31, 2026, at an agreed upon amount not to exceed $19,999.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-017-26":"Resolution No. IF-017-26\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC WORKS\nCAPITAL ACCOUNT BUDGET MODIFICATIONS\nWHEREAS, the County has identified excess SLFRF funds that were previously obligated as of the deadline, but ultimately not expended on an eligible activity, and\nWHEREAS, the County may reclassify the SLFRF funds from the original activity to another project that would be eligible under the SLFRF program rules, including the requirement that the recipient incurred an obligation by December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-220-24 allocated capital reserve funds to the Public Works Storm Facility, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-177-25 dated December 2nd, 2025, reduced the contract for Scott Lawn Yard, in which $1,143.22 of the reduction should have been applied to Oppenheim Park Improvements (H771) rather than Krull Park Improvements (H748), and\nWHEREAS, a portion these funds are covering ARPA eligible obligations that were in place prior to the December 31, 2024 deadline, and\nWHEREAS, the Treasurer's Office has identified the need for a capital project to track various upgrades, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the ARPA funds available for reclassification, in the amount of$70,557.26, be applied to the Public Storm Facility, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that $50,000 be applied to Tax Upgrades (H820) and the remaining $20,557.26 be returned to the original funding source of Capital Reserve, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH771.15.7110.000  44089.07  Federal Aid, Other ARP A State/\nLocal Fiscal Recovery  $  1,143.22\nH523.15.1620.000  44089.07  Federal Aid, Other ARPA State/\nLocal Fiscal Recovery  $ 70,557.26\nH820.07.1989.000  45031.10  Interfund Transfers from Capital\nReserves  $ 50,000.00\nDECREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH748.15.7110.000  44089.07  Federal Aid, Other ARPA State/\nLocal Fiscal Recovery  $  1,143.22\nH523.15. l 620.000  45031.10  Interfund Transfers from Capital\nReserves  $ 70,557.26\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH771.15.7110.000  72400.00  Land Improvement Expense  $  1,143.22\nH820.07.1989.000  72100.26  Technology Expense  $ 50,000.00\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH748.15.7110.000  72400.00  Land Improvement Expense  $  1,143.22\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-001-26":"Resolution No. IL-001-26\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LUMBER CITY CHURCH\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Lumber City Church mission is to serve the people of the beloved \"Lumber City\", North Tonawanda, and\nWHEREAS, the Lumber City Church has launched Nourishment for Learning, a weekend backpack food program for children who rely on school meals during the week and may not have reliable access to food on the weekends, and\nWHEREAS, the Lumber City Church is filling and delivering 10 weekend food backpacks to each of the six elementary schools in the area, a total of 60 backpacks every week, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Legislature wishes to support this initiative to help ensure that no child in the community has to worry where their next meal is coming from over the weekend, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Lumber City Church program by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nLumber City Church - Nourishment for Learning  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-002-26":"Resolution No. IL-002-26\nFrom: Legislators Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TONA WANDAS GATEWAY HARBOR INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Tonawandas Gateway Harbor, Inc. is a 501 (c)3 non-profit organization with a mission to promote economic growth, tourism, recreation, and cultural and historic heritage through the utilization of Gateway Harbor Park, and\nWHEREAS, in 2026 the Tonawandas Gateway Harbor, Inc. will once again be hosting the Gateway Harbor Wednesdays Concerts on the Canal Series, at Gateway Harbor Park, and\nWHEREAS, this concert series draws thousands of people to downtown North Tonawanda on a weekly basis, which results in a positive economic impact for area restaurants and businesses, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Tonawandas Gateway Harbor, Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nTonawandas Gateway Harbor, Inc.  $5,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-003-26":"Resolution No. IL-003-26\nFrom: Legislators Anthony J. Nemi, Randy R. Bradt, Richard L. Andres, Irene M. Myers, Richard E. Abbott, Christopher J. McKimmie, David E. Godfrey, Jesse P. Gooch, Michael A. Casale, Christopher A. Robins, Carla L. Speranza and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE NIAGARA FRONTIER LEAGUE VS.\nERIE COUNTY INTERSCHOLASTIC CONFERENCE BASKETBALL TOURNAMENT\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Frontier League (NFL) and the Erie County Interscholastic Conference (ECIC) compete in an annual basketball tournament showcasing the talent of local high school students, from across Western New York, and\nWHEREAS, athletes from Niagara County school districts, including Niagara Wheatfield, North Tonawanda, Starpoint, Lockport and Lewiston-Porter take part in this event, and\nWHEREAS, the Starpoint Central School District is hosting the 2026 tournament, and\nWHEREAS, this tournament is highly competitive, and the players look forward to participating in it each year, and\nWHEREAS, events such as this, would not be possible with community engagement and assistance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Patinership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it futiher\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the NFL vs. ECIC Basketball Tournament by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nStarpoint Central School District  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-004-26":"Resolution No. IL-004-26\nFrom: Legislator Michael A. Casale and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE INAUGURAL NIAGARA COUNTY ITALIAN FESTIVAL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Italian Festival organizers have armounced the first-ever countywide Italian Festival to be held at Academy Park in Lewiston, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Italian Festival which will take place July 17-19, 2026, will celebrate Italian culture, food, music and tradition, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Italian Festival will also celebrate the great accomplishment of the many residents of Italian heritage, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Italian Festival is another wonderful event for the Village of Lewiston and all of Niagara County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the first-ever countywide Italian Festival (Pax Romana) by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNiagara County Italian Festival (Pax Romana)  $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-005-26":"Resolution No. IL-005-26\nFrom: Legislators Christopher A, Robins, Christopher J. McKimmie and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE MENTAL HEALTH ASSOCIATION IN NIAGARA COUNTY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Mental Health Association in Niagara County is a 501 (c)3 non-profit organization that has been proudly serving the community since 1964, and\nWHEREAS, the Mental Health Association of Niagara County offers free and low-cost programs that promote mental wellness, provide needed services for those with a mental health diagnosis, and actively work to alleviate the stigma and public misunderstanding of mental illness, and\nWHEREAS, the Mental Health Association in Niagara County is a valuable resource for Niagara County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Paiinership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it fmiher\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Mental Health Association in Niagara County by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nMental Health Association in Niagara County  $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-006-26":"Resolution No. IL-006-26\nFrom: Legislator Richard L. Andres, Randy R. Bradt and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE KNIGHTS OF COLUMBUS\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus in North Tonawanda was established in 1924, and\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus was founded on the principles of charity, unity and fraternity, and\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus building is used for many functions such as banquets, weddings, bingo, etc. where these profits go to help those in need in the community, and\nWHEREAS, the Knights of Columbus are requesting funds to help with the cost of a new security system, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Knights of Columbus appropriating momes from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nKnights of Columbus  $4,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-007-26":"Resolution No. IL-007-26\nFrom: Legislators Jesse P. Gooch, Christopher J. McKimmie and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE HEARTS IN BLOOM BUFFALO, INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Hearts in Bloom Buffalo, Inc. is a 501 (c) 3 non-profit organization created to bring comfort to widows and widowers on Valentine's Day, these are individuals who often grieve quietly during this holiday, and\nWHEREAS, each year the volunteers deliver flowers and gift bags directly to their doors, reminding them they are seen, remembered and loved, and\nWHEREAS, this will be the third annual Valentine's delivery, servicing over 200 recipients across WNY, a large number right in North Tonawanda and Wheatfield area, and\nWHEREAS, Hearts in Bloom Buffalo, Inc. is looking for support in helping spread love and compassion, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of Hearts in Bloom Buffalo, Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nHearts in Bloom Buffalo, Inc.  $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-008-26":"Resolution No. IL-008-26\nFrom: Legislator Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE PROJECT FUTURE CENTER\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Project Future Center is a non-profit organization, and\nWHEREAS, the Project Future Center teaches students moral principles such as respect, discipline, confidence and strength as they receive training in Bushido Kai, and\nWHEREAS, the Project Future Center is requesting funds for a new furnace, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Project Future Center by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nThe Project Future Center  $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-009-26":"Resolution No. IL-009-26\nFrom: Legislators Anthony J. Nemi, Richard E. Abbott, Carla L. Speranza, Michael A. Hill, Shawn A Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF HISTORIC PALA CE INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Historic Palace Theatre has been a mainstay in the City of Lockport since it opened in 1925, and\nWHEREAS, Historic Palace Inc. provides a variety of family-friendly events for residents and visitors in the City of Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, Historic Palace Inc., is a community-based organization dedicated to maintaining the historic and architectural integrity of the theatre, while providing a venue for performing arts and a center for arts education, and\nWHEREAS, Historic Palace Inc. is seeking community support for the purchase of new boilers to ensure that the theatre is properly temperature-controlled for the enjoyment of its patrons, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support the effmts of Historic Palace Inc. to maintain this historic landmark, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Historic Palace Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nHistoric Palace Inc.  $8,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-010-26":"Resolution No. IL-010-26\nFrom: Legislators Ricard E. Abbott, Anthony J. Nemi, Carla L. Speranza, Jeffrey Elder, Christopher A. Robins, Sam R. Farruggia, Randy R. Bradt, Richard L. Andres, Michael A. Hill, Shawn A. Foti, Irene M. Myers, Jesse P. Gooch, Christopher J. McKimmie, David E. Godfrey, Michael A. Casale.\nDated: January 20, 2026\nA LOCAL LAW AMENDING LOCAL LAW NO. 1 FOR THE YEAR 2023\nAPPLICABLE TO THE REAL PROPERTY TAX EXEMPTION\nFOR PERSONS SIXTY-FIVE YEARS OF AGE OR OVER\nWHEREAS, Legislators Richard E. Abbott and Anthony J. Nemi present in writing the following proposed Local Law: A Local Law amending Local Law No. 1 for the year 2023 applicable to the real property tax exemption for persons sixty-five years of age or over:\nBe it enacted by the Legislature of the County of Niagara as follows:\n1.  Local Law No. I for the year 2023, and the same hereby is, amended so as to increase the\nincome levels for the real property tax exemption for persons sixty-five years of age or older from a base of $26,200.00 - $33,700.00 to $26,900.00-$34,400.00:\nSection I.  Real property owned by one or more persons, each of whom is sixty-five\nyears of age or over, or real property owned by husband and wife, or by siblings, one of whom is sixty-five years of age or over, shall be exempt from taxation for County purposes to the extent as provided in the following schedule:\nANNUAL INCOME  EXEMPTION PERCENT\nLess than:  $26,900  50%\nFrom:  $26,900  to $27,899.99  45%\n$27,900  to  $28,899.99  40%\n$28,900  to  $29,899.99  35%\n$29,900  to  $30,799.99  30%\n$30,800  to  $31,699.99  25%\n$31,700  to  $32,599.99  20%\n$32,600  to  $33,499.99  15%\n$33,500  to $34,400.00  10%\n2.  This Local Law shall become effective March 1, 2026 and this schedule shall be employed by all\nNiagara County assessing units in the preparation of any assessment roll prepared subsequent to such date.\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature shall conduct a public hearing upon said proposed Local Law at the Legislative Chambers, Niagara County Courthouse, Lockport, New York on the 17th day of February, 2026 at 5:45 p.m., and be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature, at least six (6) days in advance of such hearing, shall post a notice upon the bulletin boards in the Courthouse in Lockp011, the Civic Building in Niagara Falls shall publish such notice once in the Union Sun & Journal, and the Niagara Gazette; such notice shall contain the title of the Local Law and an abstract of the text to be prepared by the Clerk of the Legislature, with the assistance of the County Attorney's Office.\nMoved by Abbott, seconded by N emi, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pe1tains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Abbott, seconded by Nemi.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent."};
